<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismund, Archduke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1427">1427</a>–<a href="/years/1496">1496</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sigismund of Tyrol · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismund, Archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and Further Austria from 1446 to 1490. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from the mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schwaz">Schwaz</a> made him rich, earning him the name 'rich in coin'. His quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa">Nicholas of Cusa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>, brought him excommunication, and in 1490 he handed his lands to his cousin Maximilian I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-archduke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Sigismund, Archduke of Austria" width="320" height="577" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilfredor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzherzog_Siegmund_von_%C3%96sterreich_(Siegmund_der_M%C3%BCnzreiche),_Wandbild_im_Spanischen_Saal_auf_Schloss_Ambras_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigismund was born on 26 October 1427 at the Tyrolean court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, the son of Duke Frederick IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> of the Further Austrian line and his second wife Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. He was a minor when his father died in 1439, and his first cousin Frederick V acted as regent until 1446. Frederick, elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1440, used the regency to extend his influence in the Further Austrian lands, and the income from the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> remained important to him. Sigismund married Princess Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, daughter of King James I, on 8 September 1449.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sigismund took over Further Austria, which included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundgau">Sundgau</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> and smaller estates, in 1446. For much of his reign he disputed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa">Nicholas of Cusa</a>, Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>, over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a>, Puster and Inn valleys. In 1460, when he marched on the bishop's residence at Bruneck Castle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> excommunicated him. Nicholas died in 1464 before Sigismund submitted to obtain a papal pardon.</p><p>In 1469 he sold his Swabian lands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> landgraviate, Pfirt and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Sources are unclear whether he did so to pay debts from a luxurious way of life or only lent them for better protection against the Swiss. In 1477 the Emperor Frederick raised him to archduke. In 1484 he married the 16-year-old Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>; neither marriage produced children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under a coinage reform of the late 1470s and early 1480s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> were revived, and the large silver guldengroschen was struck there. It was later developed into the thaler, ancestor of many European coins and of the US dollar. In March 1487 he entered a pointless war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> after imprisoning 130 Venetian merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bozen</a>, and it ended with no decisive victory. By 1490 Tyrolean nobles compelled him to hand rule to Frederick's son Maximilian, though it is unclear whether he did so willingly. He died on 4 March 1496, and the Tyrolean branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> died out, leaving Maximilian sole heir.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigismund, Archduke of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cardinal Nicholas of Cusa, bishop from 1450, clashed bitterly with Duke <b>Sigismund of Tyrol</b> and was imprisoned in 1460.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-brixen">Prince-Bishopric of Brixen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1471" aria-label="Innsbruck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4817 505 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sigismund, Archduke of Austria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5267 775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5267 775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1471">What was here in 1471? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
